# Clinical document read scope, printed prescription identity, authoring allowlist and document numbering (rule 227)

**Status:** scope of `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-8B` (2026-11-07). Sprint record:
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-8b.md`. Owner decisions D1, D2, D6 and D7 of the Program 8 readiness re-audit,
each answered "FIX NOW" on 2026-11-07. Category under the Foundation-First Sprint Lock: security fix / clinical
correctness / financial correctness.

This rule EXTENDS rule 226. Where they differ, this rule wins: rule 226 §1.3 ("refused the same way") was an overclaim
until §3 below, §1.6 now also covers the printed names (§2), and §1.7 no longer lists P8-N2 as undecided (§1). Rule 225
owns the advisory-lock pattern §4 reuses.

## 1. Clinical documents have their own read scope (D1, P8-N2)

1. The medical record and its handwriting images, the prescription (page, R/ and signature canvases, print, history
   list beside the form) and the odontogram (page, view, print) are read under
   `RmeWorkingBranchScope::clinicalDocumentBranchIdsFor()` / `::allowsClinicalDocument()`. Nothing else decides it.
2. A context-bound account that holds NO clinical role — Kasir, Admin Klinik, Front Office — reads clinical documents
   only in its working branch, and none without a working context (fail closed: an empty scope, never MAIN, never the
   whole estate). That is the same scope the visit itself is read under.
3. Every other account keeps exactly the RME-enabled set it had: clinicians (Doctor, Perawat, Supervisor RME, Super
   Admin) stay cross-branch because a patient's record book spans the branches they were treated in; Owner and the
   reporting roles are unchanged. A clinical role wins over a front-desk or cashier role on the same account.
   **SUPERSEDED in part by rule 228 §5 (DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-8C, D3-NEW-4):** the cross-branch read is now an
   allowlist (clinical roles + Owner); any other account reads no clinical document until it is classified.
4. The scope only ever NARROWS. Every answer is a subset of `BranchService::rmeEnabledIds()`; a `null` user reads
   nothing. The doctor branch lock does not reach it (same reason as `allows()`), and the doctor-patient scope still
   applies on top in the policies.
5. The unified medical-record index's native rows use the same scope, so a list never shows a record its reader
   cannot open. `RmePrescriptionService::showDataForVisit()` takes the viewer as a REQUIRED argument so no caller can
   fall back to the whole estate by omission.
6. Write abilities are not changed by this section; writes are governed by the authoring authorities (rule 223 §3,
   rule 226, §3 below).
7. Consequence, accepted with D1: a front-desk account cannot open the RM workspace of a patient whose canonical record
   (Sprint 64.0.2 — the pages live on the earliest visit's record) belongs to another branch. Its own branch's "Cetak RME"
   (governed by `ClinicVisitPolicy::print`) is unchanged.
8. The read-only oversight module (`view_clinical_records_readonly`, Finance Klinik / Data Analyst) has its own branch
   scope (rule 186) and is not changed here.
8a. Handwriting is attributed to the branch that owns the patient's canonical record book, not to the branch of the
    encounter it was written in: Sprint 64.0.2 stores every page on the canonical record and stamps the page with that
    record's branch, and no page carries its encounter branch. So the document scope of a handwriting image is the
    canonical record's branch. Changing that needs a recorded encounter branch per page (a schema change) and is an
    owner decision (D3), not part of D1.
8b. Not covered by D1 (recorded for D3): the RM workspace still lists other branches' sheet metadata (visit numbers,
    dates, page timestamps) to a front-desk account whose canonical record is in its own branch — the bytes are
    refused; and D1 narrows the context-bound non-clinical roles only, so a non-context-bound role created at runtime
    keeps the previous RME-wide read (never wider than before).

## 2. The printed prescription names come from the clinical records (D6, P8R-S3)

9. `prescribed_by_name` and `patient_name_snapshot` are NOT request fields. Both FormRequests do not accept them; the
   form shows them read-only with no input; `old()` is never used for them.
10. `RmePrescriptionService` derives them server-side: on create from the visit's doctor and patient (and `doctor_id`
    from that same doctor); on update from the prescription's OWN doctor and patient, falling back to the visit's only
    when the row never recorded one, so the printed name and `doctor_id` are always the same person (a later room
    re-assignment that rewrites the visit doctor, OD-5-3, does not change who the prescription was written under).
11. A prescription is refused when there is no doctor (or no patient) to name — before any canvas is decoded or
    written. Never print under an invented or blank name.
11a. The snapshot is read through the soft-deleted rows too (`withTrashed()`): a doctor or patient whose master record
     was archived still names their past visits' prescriptions.
11b. D6 fixes WHO IS NAMED, not WHO SIGNS: an authoring role other than the named doctor (a nurse, a covering
     dentist) may still draw the signature canvas printed above the visit doctor's name; only `created_by` records the
     real author. Restricting the signature to the named doctor conflicts with OD-5-1 (Perawat / Supervisor RME may
     author) and is an owner decision (D3).
12. The print renders the stored snapshot. Existing rows are not rewritten by a deploy (production 2026-11-07: 2 rows,
    both snapshots already equal to their relations); a row is re-derived only when it is next updated.

## 3. Prescription authoring is an allowlist plus the permission, in the service (D7, P8R-S1, P8R-S2)

13. `PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority::permitsActorAtBranch()` requires, in order: the write permission
    `manage_clinic_visits` itself (`can()`, so `Gate::before` still answers for Super Admin); not the front-desk-only
    tier; a role in `AUTHORING_ROLES` (= `FrontOfficeRole::CLINICAL_ROLES`: Doctor, Perawat, Supervisor RME, Super
    Admin); the working branch. The policy and the service both read it, so rule 226 §1.3 is now true.
14. It is an ALLOWLIST on purpose. The front-desk refusal is a denylist of two roles; a role created at runtime on the
    role screen and given `manage_clinic_visits` (production holds one such role, no holders) would otherwise inherit
    prescription authoring with nobody deciding it. A CI test over RoleSeeder cannot see a runtime role. A new role that
    must write prescriptions is added to the allowlist on purpose, with its own review.
15. Role-less accounts holding the permission directly are refused (production 2026-11-07: zero direct grants of
    `manage_clinic_visits`). Test suites that need a prescription author use `rmeClinicalAuthor()`.
16. `RmeVisitConsentService::actorMayWorkOnEncounter()` (RME and odontogram authoring) re-asserts the same write
    permission, so Kasir — context-bound but not front-desk — is refused by the service and the UI capability, not only
    by the route. Whether RME/odontogram authoring also takes the role allowlist is an owner decision (D3), not made
    here.
17. `RmePrescriptionAuthoringRoleMatrixTest` pins the full matrix over every seeded role, a runtime-created role, a
    role-less account, a clinical role without the permission, direct service calls, and a classification guard: every
    RoleSeeder role holding `manage_clinic_visits` is clinical or front desk.

## 4. RME invoice, payment and consent numbers are serialised (D2, P8-N4, OD-7-8, OD-5-8)

18. `RME-YYYYMM-NNNNNN`, `RMEPAY-YYYYMM-NNNNNN` and `CONSENT-YYYYMM-NNNNNN` are each chosen after
    `DocumentNumberSequenceLock::acquire()` — `pg_advisory_xact_lock(namespace, YYYYMM)` — taken through the
    repository's `lockNumberSequence($month)` before the month's highest number is read. Namespaces: 92_002 invoice,
    92_003 payment, 92_004 consent (92_001 is the visit sequence, 91_xxx the legacy slot). NEVER renumber one: during a
    rolling deploy an old and a new process would take different locks for the same month.
19. Outside a transaction the lock is a `LogicException`, not a silent no-op; on a non-PostgreSQL driver it is a no-op
    (SQLite serialises writers at the file level). The objid is the exact integer YYYYMM, never a hash.
20. Invoice and payment MAX reads use `withTrashed()`: the UNIQUE indexes still hold a soft-deleted row's number, so
    ignoring it would reissue that number and wedge the whole month on HTTP 500. Consents have no soft delete; a voided
    consent keeps its number.
21. Nothing is renumbered. Every issued number keeps its value; the month prefixes are unchanged (the invoice and
    payment prefixes still use `now()` — P8R-S6 is a D3 item).
22. Lock order is unchanged and deadlock-free: every caller already holds its row locks (visit, invoice) before the
    generator runs, and the three namespaces never wait for each other.

## 5. Critical-gate coverage (CI-8B)

23. The four suites are declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites` and selected in BOTH
    critical-gate variants (rule 120, CG-19): new tokens `RmeClinicalDocumentReadScope` and
    `RmeDocumentNumberSequenceLock` (before the pinned calendar-boundary tail, rule 212); `RmePrescriptionPrintedIdentity`
    and `RmePrescriptionAuthoringRoleMatrix` are selected by the existing `RmePrescription` token, which now selects four
    files instead of rule 226 §12's two. The lock suite's PostgreSQL cases SKIP on SQLite; they ran on PostgreSQL 16.15.
